# Global extension — `IExtensionGlobal`

## When to use

Code must run **without anyone clicking anything**: once while ENCY starts, once while it shuts down.
Typical work: start a background service, subscribe to application-level events, warm a cache, read a
company config, register something other extensions rely on later.

Not this kind if:
- the user is supposed to trigger the action → `type-utility.mdc`;
- you only need a menu entry somewhere → the popup guides (`type-operation-popup.mdc`,
  `type-geom-node-popup.mdc`).

A global extension has no UI surface of its own. If it opens a window on startup, users cannot get rid
of it — think twice.

## Register it

The key is `Global` (upstream examples capitalise it; ENCY matches case-insensitively):

```json
{
  "name": "My startup hooks",
  "description": "What it does",
  "module_path": "${extensionJsonFolder}/MyExtension.dll",
  "extensions": [
    {
      "Global": {
        "id": "Extension.Global.MyExtension",
        "name": "Runs on application start and finish"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

No `caption`/`icon_path` — nothing is drawn. The `id` must match the branch in the factory:

```csharp
// src/ExtensionFactory.cs — class stays CAMAPI.ExtensionFactory
"Extension.Global.MyExtension" => new GlobalExtension(),
```

## Interface

```csharp
public interface IExtension       { IExtensionInfo? Info { get; set; } }
public interface IExtensionGlobal { TResultStatus OnSCInitializing();
                                   TResultStatus OnSCFinalizing(); }
```

No context is handed to you. Reach the application through `ICamApiApplicationSingleton` (the
singleton mechanism) when you need `ICamApiApplication` — active project, extension manager, tools
manager, machines library, utility manager, macro manager.

## Skeleton

```csharp
using CAMAPI.Application;
using CAMAPI.Extensions;
using CAMAPI.ResultStatus;

namespace MyExtension;

public class GlobalExtension : IExtension, IExtensionGlobal
{
    public IExtensionInfo? Info { get; set; }

    public TResultStatus OnSCInitializing()
    {
        TResultStatus status = default;
        try
        {
            // Cheap setup only. Reach the app via ICamApiApplicationSingleton if needed.
        }
        catch (Exception e)
        {
            status.Code = TResultStatusCode.rsError;
            status.Description = e.Message;
        }
        return status;
    }

    public TResultStatus OnSCFinalizing()
    {
        TResultStatus status = default;
        try
        {
            // Release everything taken in OnSCInitializing: timers, COM refs, files, sockets.
        }
        catch (Exception e)
        {
            status.Code = TResultStatusCode.rsError;
            status.Description = e.Message;
        }
        return status;
    }
}
```

## Gotchas

- **`OnSCInitializing` holds ENCY's startup.** No network calls, no heavy scans, no dialogs — move
  work to a background task and let the application finish starting.
- **The UI may not exist yet.** Anything window-related on this stage is a race; do it lazily on
  first use instead.
- **Symmetry is on you.** Whatever `OnSCInitializing` takes, `OnSCFinalizing` must release — a leaked
  COM reference or a live timer keeps the library loaded and blocks reinstall/update.
- **Exceptions must not leave either method.** Catch and report via `TResultStatus`
  (`TResultStatusCode.rsError`).
- **One global per dll is normally enough.** Multiple globals in one library just multiply startup
  cost; use one and dispatch internally.

